WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:10.040
Students across the land are wrapping up
annual state tests. For all the backlash

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:15.440
these tests spark, they exist mostly in
response to a sensible expectation that

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.599
parents know how their schools are doing.
We should neither abandon testing nor

00:00:20.599 --> 00:00:24.169
ignore its problems, but ensure that it's
used responsibly.

00:00:24.169 --> 00:00:28.999
Here are four thoughts on how to do just
that. First, reading and math scores can

00:00:28.999 --> 00:00:33.800
go up or down for lots of reasons. Why
scores go up matters as much as whether

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.270
they do. Second, when a focus on reading
and math tests shifts time away from

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:42.860
other important learning, we need to
weigh the costs and benefits. Third,

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:47.810
reading and math tests capture only a
narrow slice of what schools are asked

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:52.520
to do. That matters. Fourth, today's state
tests don't provide meaningful immediate

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:56.780
feedback. It's hard to blame families for
doubting the value of tests which are

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.550
more useful for grading schools than for
educating kids. Look, testing is a

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:06.640
valuable tool, but like any tool, it's
only effective when it's used well.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:11.600
How do you think we can better use
standardized tests? Let us know in your

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.280
comments. Also, let us know what other
topics you'd like our scholars to cover

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:21.920
in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and
subscribe for more research and videos

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.030
from AEI.

